{
 "result": "Built maze.py: a BFS shortest-path solver for '#'/'.' grids with S and E, 4-neighbour. It embeds a self-contained C program (compiled on first use to .maze_bin_v1 and cached/re-exec'd, with a pure-Python BFS fallback): the C code reads all of stdin in one growing buffer, derives width/height from the raw bytes (handling \\r\\n and a missing trailing newline), builds a padded (W+2)x(H+2) byte grid, locates S/E, and runs a level-order BFS with a flat int distance array and a preallocated ring queue, printing the distance or -1.\n\nVerification: `python3 tests.py` runs 250 cases (hand-written edge cases \u2014 no path, same-cell, single row with no trailing newline, CRLF input \u2014 plus 240 randomized small mazes at varying wall densities) against an independent Python reference, reporting 0 mismatches. The registered assay `python3 bench.py` uses a fixed-seed 2000x2000 maze (4 MB), 15 timed reps, checks exact output each rep: current score 15494.97 vs baseline 15425.15, consistent with noise (spread ~4%), i.e. startup/IO-bound at ~65 ms per run where a bare Python interpreter start is ~23 ms and the compiled core alone is ~43 ms.",
